    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Who we introduce you to</h1>
      <p class="lead">We work with several data buyers and AI labs. Once a company is introduced, more than one of them looks at the dataset and the terms are compared.</p>
      <p><a class="btn" href="contact.html">Tell us about your data</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section mist">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What the buyers do</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card reveal">
        <span class="icon-tile" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-search"/></svg></span>
        <h3>Assess the opportunity</h3>
          <p>Each looks at what your data could support and whether there is a real market for it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card reveal">
        <span class="icon-tile" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-chat"/></svg></span>
        <h3>Run their own diligence</h3>
          <p>Rights, personal data and scope are checked inside each buyer, not argued with you.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card reveal">
        <span class="icon-tile" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><use href="icons.svg#i-check"/></svg></span>
        <h3>Bring you terms, not one offer</h3>
          <p>More than one desk sets a price, so you see what the market will actually pay. Nothing is agreed without you.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head reveal">
        <p class="kicker">One dataset, several desks</p>
        <h2>One dataset, more than one buyer.</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="reveal">A support archive, a document set and a deal history are worth different amounts to different AI teams. Rather than taking the first offer, we put the opportunity in front of the buyers we work with and compare what comes back on price, scope and term. A buyer that passes on one category often wants another.</p>
      <p class="reveal">You still approve every buyer before anything is shared, and each agreement is between your company and the buyer you accept.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">What it does not do</h2>
      <p class="reveal">The introduction changes who carries the conversation, not what you agree to.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Skip the rights review</strong>Every record set is checked before a buyer sees it, and the scope is set in writing first.</li>
        <li><strong>Share anything on your behalf</strong>Nothing leaves your systems until you have approved the terms.</li>
        <li><strong>Decide for you</strong>You approve every step, and you can stop at any point.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="link-row reveal"><a href="trust.html">How we handle your data</a> <a href="what-affects-a-deal.html">What affects a deal</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">Why an AI deal is not a normal data licence</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A standard licence names a purpose and stops. The records here can spawn new material, so the terms have to say what happens at each layer: cleaned datasets, annotations, embeddings, evaluation sets, fine-tuned models, model weights and outputs.</p>
      <p class="reveal">That work belongs to the buyers rather than to you, but it explains why the conversation is specific about purpose and scope long before anyone discusses a price.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

